INT. SALT MINE CHAPEL - NIGHT

A cathedral carved from rock salt, a thousand feet below the earth. Chandeliers of crystal hang from a vaulted ceiling. The walls glow faint pink in the lamplight. Pews of carved salt face an altar whose carved relief shows the Last Supper, every face worn smooth by humidity.

DETECTIVE AMARA OSEI-LINDQVIST (40s, hard hat over a bun, heavy coat, notebook sealed in a plastic bag) stands at the altar rail, holding a flashlight. Her breath hangs in the cold.

FATHER BENEDIKT WRÓBLEWSKI (60s, black cassock, miner's lamp strapped to his forehead, a rosary wound around one gnarled hand) kneels at the altar's foot, staring at the floor.

Between them, on the salt floor, a dark stain.

AMARA
Father, I need you to look at me.

BENEDIKT
(to the stain)
I have been looking at it for six hours.

AMARA
Then look at me.

He lifts his head slowly. His eyes are clear and wet.

AMARA (CONT'D)
When did you last see Brother Tomasz?

BENEDIKT
At Vespers.

AMARA
Alone?

BENEDIKT
No.

AMARA
Who was with him?

Benedikt does not answer. He turns the rosary beads, one at a time. Somewhere in the dark, a pump coughs.

AMARA (CONT'D)
Father.

BENEDIKT
(whispering)
God forgive me.

AMARA
Not yet. First, tell me what you saw.

BENEDIKT
(louder, to the empty pews)
I saw nothing! I saw nothing at all!

His voice echoes off the carved walls. Salt sifts from the ceiling like snow. Amara waits until it settles.

AMARA
(quietly)
There are only two ways into this chapel. The lift, and the old shaft. I've checked both.

BENEDIKT
Then you know.

AMARA
I know the lift has been locked since midnight.

BENEDIKT
And the shaft?

AMARA
Someone used it. Recently. There are fresh boot marks in the brine.

Benedikt closes his eyes.

AMARA (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Cassock hem was wet when I came in.

BENEDIKT
What?

AMARA
Nothing.

She steps around the stain, studying the angle of the carved altar. The flashlight beam finds a smear of red on the base of the relief, a small, deliberate handprint at about shoulder height.

AMARA (CONT'D)
That's not blood.

BENEDIKT
It is paint.

AMARA
Why would there be paint on the altar?

BENEDIKT
Brother Tomasz was restoring the faces.

AMARA
Which ones?

BENEDIKT
The apostles.

She raises the light. Twelve faces, worn blank. One of them, third from the left, has been freshly carved: gaunt, kind, familiar.

Benedikt looks up. His mouth opens, closes.

AMARA
Father. That face is yours.

BENEDIKT
(barely audible)
Yes.

AMARA
Why would he carve you into the altar?

BENEDIKT
Because I asked him to.

AMARA
Why?

BENEDIKT
Because I wanted to be remembered.

The pump coughs again. Amara lowers the flashlight. The chapel plunges into gentle pink dark, and the two figures stand in it, listening to the salt settle.
